The proposed 10-message plan is accepted as Slice A: Understand and place the message.

Prepare one read-only companion plan, Slice B: Complete the layers.

A person may enter at product intent, taste/design, architecture, test, code or infrastructure. Preserve the supplied layer as authoritative, derive missing adjacent layers, invoke only relevant skills, and converge on one linked outcome.

Use one small representative feature and five independent entry messages:

1. product behavior wish;
2. taste/design concern;
3. architecture constraint;
4. request for a test;
5. code/infrastructure observation, including a browser/Chrome case.

For each show:
- supplied layer;
- relation to existing work;
- missing layers;
- selected and skipped skills with reasons;
- actual product decisions requiring the person;
- derived claims and evidence;
- final spec → architecture → matrix/test → implementation links;
- plain-language echo.

Preservation constraints, outside the implementation scope of Slice B:

- timestamps, task status, worker heartbeat, blockers, handoffs and receipts must later derive from one timestamped event spine rather than prose reminders;
- preserve the exercised inbox exchange and agent-card/contracts as later capabilities;
- preserve the route “host finding → reusable candidate → reproduction/scope/provenance/consent → shared template or skill”;
- every new or substantively changed skill must later prove the portable skill lifecycle: relevant evals, actual skill-creator-adapter execution, per-finding disposition, version/install report and rollback; a review marker alone is insufficient;
- direct agent chat, automatic roster, wake-on-change and silent self-promotion stay later;
- do not turn these preservation constraints into new Slice-B implementation.

Success: all five entries converge without duplicate tasks, lost intent or ceremonial full-pipeline work. Routine technical completion is handled; a genuine product fork is asked.

Return:
1. Slice B plan;
2. combined implementation order for Step 0, Slice A and Slice B;
3. a compact preservation ledger mapping the constraints above to their existing canonical homes and later roadmap stage.

Maximum 1,200 words. Read-only. No edits, tests, files, worktrees, commits or push.

Slice B is accepted with corrections. Do not edit yet.

Revise the combined execution plan once, read-only, then stop architecture work.

Corrections:

1. The Chrome/keyboard entry must run against a browser-capable host or fixture with a real browser surface. LiveSpec’s own pack remains a harness/template oracle, not proof that the pack itself exercises a browser UI.

2. Do not impose “exactly two” questions or any invented numeric target. Ask the person only for genuine product or taste forks. Routine completion proceeds without asking.

3. Success is one stable related feature/task identity across all five entry messages, with dated refinements and no duplicate candidate. Do not use a queue-row-count target as the product measure.

4. Introduce the minimum timestamped event relation in Slice A/B:
message received → outcome → related task/candidate → plain-language echo → timestamp/provenance.
Do not wait for the full work-board generator. The later board, monitoring, receipts, heartbeats and estimates will project from this event spine.

5. Add these non-negotiable contracts to the execution plan:
- chat follows the user’s language and desired detail;
- canonical product, architecture, matrix/test, migration, skill, commit and published-artifact documents are natural technical English;
- each document type has its own audience, structure, fact boundary, load tier and readability proof;
- explanations lead with human task name, larger goal, current state and next safe action; codes/paths/mechanisms stay in details;
- orchestration is lean and agentic: the orchestrator keeps goals, work state, decisions, reservations and concise evidence summaries; workers do bulk reads/drafts/reviews/implementation;
- use isolated worktrees for independent write sets and one integration owner for shared canonical documents;
- dynamic capacity may use up to 20 workers when budget, independent work, worktrees and shared resources permit;
- model selection is configuration chosen by task complexity, risk, context/tool need and budget; provider model names are not product law;
- every execution wave ends with one user-readable digest grouped by product task, not worker logs.

Return the final execution order:
Step 0 repairs → Slice A → Slice B → acceptance gates → next capability packages.

For each stage state:
- user-visible outcome;
- exact canonical documents/contracts touched;
- maximum safe parallelism and worktree boundaries;
- worker roles;
- integration owner responsibility;
- focused proofs;
- stop condition.

Maximum 1,200 words. No new design branches, no edits, tests, worktrees, commits or push.

Do one final read-only implementation-readiness correction, then stop planning.

The execution order is accepted with these required corrections:

1. Event record:
Before Slice A defines “the host event record”, specify its exact local path, minimal schema, owner, task identity reference, append/concurrency protocol, retention/redaction policy and migration behavior.

It is append-only local evidence, not a second task authority. Queue/work graph owns task state; the event record references it. Raw chat/secrets do not enter it.

2. Step 0 commit boundaries:
Do not make Step 0 one large atomic commit. Split it into small reversible commits:
- K1 migration-chain repair;
- K2 queue/status and archive repair;
- K3 stale-row repair;
- K4 source-side external-clone sync safety;
- M1 installed-copy sync only after source verification and explicit owner approval, because it changes shared machine state.

Each commit has explicit files and focused proofs. Protected dirty files remain untouched.

3. Slice B verification:
Replace “the full pipeline never runs” with:
“The route runs only the proof its actual changed layer and risk require. A broader proof runs when the conservative classifier or the touched contract requires it.”
Do not save time by suppressing a required proof.

4. Browser fixture:
Name the exact browser-capable fixture host/surface and its ownership before implementation. Do not claim the pack itself is a browser UI.

Return only:
- the corrected final execution order;
- event-record contract;
- Step 0 commit boundaries;
- focused proof commands;
- the few owner decisions still required.

Maximum 700 words. No agents, edits, tests, worktrees, commits or push.

Proceed with C-K1 only. No other Step 0 commit, M1 sync, Slice A/B, push or worktree changes.

Authorized owner decisions for C-K1:
- repair the missing 5.0.0 migration chapter using only host-visible actions proven by the released external-prover change;
- repair the stale rule-30 reference;
- mark row 602 landed and rotate/archive it with the required manifest entry;
- preserve all protected dirty files, branches and worktrees.

Do not decide or create the event record yet. `.live-spec/events.md` is not approved; event-storage design belongs immediately before Slice A.

Use one writer. You may use up to two read-only reviewers for factual verification. Make the red-first test, implement only C-K1, run the declared focused proofs, commit C-K1 with explicit paths, and stop before push.

Return:
- commit hash;
- changed files;
- exact verification results;
- remaining working-tree changes;
- no extra work.

Run a source-anchored, read-only Capability Ownership Ledger audit of LiveSpec.

Use up to 20 Explore agents only where domains are independent. Keep the coordinator lean; workers read source and return compact evidence, not dumps. Do not edit the repository, create no commits, worktrees, artifacts, plans, or published links.

For every capability, record:
capability | canonical owner | exact file and heading/line anchor | input | output | explicit boundary (“does not do”) | invocation/route | test or other proof | state: working / partial / prose-only / stale.

Cover at minimum:
1) product-prover and its class/lookalike lens;
2) design-reviewer same-kind and behaviour-parity lens;
3) spec-author, test-author, text-audit, communicator, feedback;
4) intake, queue, board, task identity and statuses;
5) onboarding, user/project policy, project parameters/defaults/design recommendations;
6) agent scheduling, worktrees, inbox/receipts/heartbeats;
7) estimates, timestamps, monitoring;
8) browser harness and reusable host learning;
9) skill lifecycle, skill-creator/evals, external-skill installation/sync;
10) migration, legacy hosts, cross-project learning.

Treat similar names as potentially different mechanisms. Do not infer ownership from prose summaries: cite source. Explicitly distinguish the prover’s factual defect sweep across lookalikes from the design-reviewer’s proposed same-kind groups and behaviour-parity recommendations.

Output one compact ledger plus:
- contradictions or duplicate authorities;
- capabilities described but not mechanically invoked;
- capabilities that are implemented but need a lighter route;
- corrections required to the current vNext roadmap.

Stop after the report. No recommendations for implementation yet.

